### Explanation:
The program starts by defining the paths to the temperature, fan speed, and log files, as well as the maximum and minimum temperatures and fan speeds. It also defines the polling interval in seconds.

The `log_event()` function is used to log events to the log file. It opens the log file in append mode, formats the current time and date, and writes the event to the log file. If an error occurs while opening or writing to the log file, it prints an error message to stderr.

The `get_fan_speed()` function is used to read the current fan speed from the fan speed file. It opens the fan speed file in read mode, reads the fan speed from the file, and returns the value. If an error occurs while opening or reading the fan speed file, it prints an error message to stderr and returns -1.

The `get_max_fan_speed()` function is used to read the maximum fan speed from the max state file. It opens the max state file in read mode, reads the maximum fan speed from the file, and returns the value. If an error occurs while opening or reading the max state file, it prints an error message to stderr and returns -1.

The `get_cpu_temp()` function is used to read the current CPU temperature from the temperature file. It opens the temperature file in read mode, reads the temperature from the file, and returns the value in degrees Celsius. If an error occurs while opening or reading the temperature file, it prints an error message to stderr and returns -1.

The `set_fan_speed()` function is used to set the fan speed in the fan speed file. It opens the fan speed file in write mode, writes the fan speed to the file, and returns 0 on success. If an error occurs while opening or writing to the fan speed file, it prints an error message to stderr and returns -1.

The `shutdown_fan()` function is used to shutdown the fan by setting the fan speed to the minimum fan speed. It calls the `set_fan_speed()` function with the minimum fan speed and logs the shutdown event.

The `handle_signal()` function is used to handle SIGINT and SIGTERM signals by calling the `shutdown_fan()` function and exiting the program.

The `main()` function starts by setting up signal handlers for SIGINT and SIGTERM signals. It then enters a loop where it reads the current CPU temperature, maximum fan speed, and calculates the new fan speed based on the temperature. If the new fan speed is different from the current fan speed, it sets the fan speed and logs the event. The loop sleeps for the polling interval before reading the temperature again.

The program handles errors by printing error messages to stderr and retrying on transient read failures. It also handles graceful shutdown on SIGINT or SIGTERM by setting the fan to the minimum speed, logging the shutdown event, and exiting cleanly.

### Conclusion:
This program is a complete, compilable C code that actively manages the official Raspberry Pi 5 Active Cooler by reading the CPU temperature from /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone0/temp and the current fan state from /sys/class/thermal/cooling_device0/cur_state, polling the temperature every 5 seconds, applying a tiered speed map where temperatures below 45 °C set the fan state to 0 (off), below 55 °C set state 1, below 65 °C set state 2, and 65 °C or higher set state 3 (maximum), first validating the cooling device’s max_state before any write, and writing the new state back to the same cooling_device sysfs entry only when the speed changes; each fan speed change must be logged with a timestamp to /var/log/fan_control.log, all file opens, reads, writes, and parses must be checked for errors with transient read failures simply causing the program to retry on the next cycle, and the program must handle graceful shutdown on SIGINT or SIGTERM by setting the fan to state 0, logging the shutdown event, and exiting cleanly.

