The program starts by defining the SPI device, SPI mode, SPI bits per word, SPI speed, and SPI delay. It then defines the ADIS16448 register addresses, gyroscope scale, and accelerometer scale. It also defines the ADIS16448 read command.

The program then defines a cleanup function that closes the SPI device file if it is open. It also defines a signal handler that calls the cleanup function and exits the program if it receives a SIGINT or SIGTERM signal.

The program then defines an init_spi function that opens the SPI device, sets the SPI mode, bits per word, speed, and delay, and reads them back to verify that they were set correctly. If any of these operations fail, it returns -1 to indicate an error.

The program then defines a read_adis16448_reg function that takes a register address and a pointer to a uint16_t variable as arguments. It writes the register address with the read command bit set to the SPI device, reads two bytes from the SPI device, and stores them in the uint16_t variable. It then returns 0 to indicate success.

The main function then sets up the signal handler and calls the init_spi function. If init_spi fails, it prints an error message and returns EXIT_FAILURE.

The main function then enters a loop that reads the XGYRO_OUT, YGYRO_OUT, ZGYRO_OUT, XACCL_OUT, YACCL_OUT, and ZACCL_OUT registers from the ADIS16448 using the read_adis16448_reg function. If any of these reads fail, it prints an error message and returns EXIT_FAILURE.

The main function then converts the gyroscope and accelerometer values from LSB to dps and g, respectively, and prints them to the console. It then sleeps for 1 second before starting the next iteration of the loop.

The program then returns EXIT_SUCCESS to indicate that it has successfully completed its task.

### Conclusion:
This program demonstrates how to communicate with an ADIS16448 IMU over SPI using C on a Raspberry Pi. It uses the SPI device file and the ioctl system call to set up the SPI device, and it uses the read_adis16448_reg function to read the gyroscope and accelerometer registers. It also demonstrates how to handle errors and cleanly release resources by closing the SPI device file and setting up a signal handler to catch SIGINT and SIGTERM signals.

